WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.120
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:05.760
SPEAKER: OS Login is a
Compute Engine service

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:08.370
that provides an easy way
to manage user permissions

00:00:08.370 --> 00:00:13.620
for accessing instances via SSH
using Compute Engine IAM roles.

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:17.040
However, having multiple roles
on different resource levels

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:20.620
can make it confusing to
figure out access issues.

00:00:20.620 --> 00:00:22.980
Let's take a look at how
to investigate SSH access

00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:27.060
issues with OS Login and the
IAM policy troubleshooter.

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:29.490
First, let's review a few
different roles for users

00:00:29.490 --> 00:00:31.310
to use OS Login.

00:00:31.310 --> 00:00:33.990
Compute.osAdminLogin
allows the user

00:00:33.990 --> 00:00:36.930
to SSH into the instance
as an administrator,

00:00:36.930 --> 00:00:39.240
while compute.osLogin
allows them

00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:41.760
to SSH in as a regular user.

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:45.030
The iam.serviceAccountUser
role allows a user

00:00:45.030 --> 00:00:48.150
to run operations as
a service account.

00:00:48.150 --> 00:00:50.290
These roles can be granted
at the project level

00:00:50.290 --> 00:00:53.190
so they can apply to
resources inside that project.

00:00:53.190 --> 00:00:55.690
They can also be granted
at a more granular level,

00:00:55.690 --> 00:00:59.200
such as an instance or a
service account directly.

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:00.760
Let's walk through an example.

00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:04.110
We'll start by trying
to SSH into myinstance-1

00:01:04.110 --> 00:01:07.080
On Instances Details page, we
can see the service account

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:08.610
for this instance.

00:01:08.610 --> 00:01:11.180
At the top of the
page, the SSH button

00:01:11.180 --> 00:01:13.830
will let us connect to the
instance, so let's go ahead

00:01:13.830 --> 00:01:14.410
and click it.

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:20.760
Looks like everything
is working well.

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:23.970
Now, let's try the same
thing with myinstance-2.

00:01:23.970 --> 00:01:25.920
We'll head over to the
Instance Details page

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:28.540
from the main menu.

00:01:28.540 --> 00:01:31.430
This instance is also running
the same service account,

00:01:31.430 --> 00:01:34.990
but when we scroll up to the
SSH option, it's greyed out.

00:01:34.990 --> 00:01:36.153
That's strange.

00:01:36.153 --> 00:01:37.570
Let's try it from
the command line

00:01:37.570 --> 00:01:40.090
instead by using the
Cloud Shell terminal

00:01:40.090 --> 00:01:43.710
and running gcloud compute SSH.

00:01:43.710 --> 00:01:45.930
We get a permission denied
error, which explains why

00:01:45.930 --> 00:01:48.000
the SSH button was greyed out.

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:50.790
But without any other
details, how can we figure out

00:01:50.790 --> 00:01:52.555
why we don't have permission?

00:01:52.555 --> 00:01:54.180
Let's evaluate the
permissions required

00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:55.620
in the policy troubleshooter.

00:01:55.620 --> 00:01:57.360
We can evaluate
multiple permissions

00:01:57.360 --> 00:01:59.790
on multiple resources
at the same time

00:01:59.790 --> 00:02:02.380
so that we can compare
the results of each.

00:02:02.380 --> 00:02:05.410
Let's check for the
compute.instances.osLogin

00:02:05.410 --> 00:02:09.090
permission on myinstance-1
and myinstance-2.

00:02:09.090 --> 00:02:10.680
This permission
comes from the roles

00:02:10.680 --> 00:02:12.180
that we mentioned earlier.

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:14.010
We'll add our user
as the principal

00:02:14.010 --> 00:02:17.700
and add the full resource
name and permission.

00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:20.760
Also, since these instances are
running as a service account,

00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:24.090
we can check for the
iam.serviceAccounts.actas

00:02:24.090 --> 00:02:26.400
permission on the
actual service account.

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:30.450
The permission is part of the
service account user role.

00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:31.950
The policy
troubleshooter shows us

00:02:31.950 --> 00:02:34.350
details on the
permissions for our users.

00:02:34.350 --> 00:02:37.770
We can change between our views
by using the dropdown here.

00:02:37.770 --> 00:02:39.930
The first view shows
us that OS Login

00:02:39.930 --> 00:02:43.920
is granted to myinstance-1, and
we can see the specific details

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:46.270
here on the right.

00:02:46.270 --> 00:02:49.020
When we switch the view
over to myinstance-2,

00:02:49.020 --> 00:02:51.870
we can see that our user doesn't
have the same permissions.

00:02:51.870 --> 00:02:54.060
Since the user doesn't
have the right permissions,

00:02:54.060 --> 00:02:55.950
they can't connect through SSH.

00:02:55.950 --> 00:02:58.750
Let's add our user by going
to the Compute Engine page.

00:02:58.750 --> 00:03:00.450
From here, we can
select the instance

00:03:00.450 --> 00:03:02.940
and grant our user the
compute.osLogin role.

00:03:06.290 --> 00:03:08.510
Now that we can see the
user has the role, let's

00:03:08.510 --> 00:03:12.220
try connecting
through SSH again.

00:03:12.220 --> 00:03:16.980
Once that's done, we can so that
the user is now able to SSH in.

00:03:16.980 --> 00:03:20.130
Another option would be to
grant the user compute.osLogin

00:03:20.130 --> 00:03:21.510
at the project
level, which would

00:03:21.510 --> 00:03:25.270
allow them to use OS Login for
all instances in the project.

00:03:25.270 --> 00:03:28.350
Keep in mind the security best
practice of least privilege,

00:03:28.350 --> 00:03:30.375
and set your
permissions accordingly.

00:03:30.375 --> 00:03:32.250
Take a look at the
documentation to read more

00:03:32.250 --> 00:03:33.417
about policy troubleshooter.

00:03:33.417 --> 00:03:36.500
[MUSIC PLAYING]

